.TH "WAITFOR" 1 "2026-09-29" "toolbelt 0.1.0" "toolbelt manual"
.SH NAME
waitfor \- wait for a port, a URL or a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
waitfor [options] HOST:PORT | URL | PATH [-- curl options]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBwaitfor\fR is for scripts that start something and must not go on until it is ready. A database container takes a few seconds before it accepts connections. A web app answers 503 while it warms up. A daemon writes its socket file a moment after it starts. \fBwaitfor\fR tries again and again until the thing is ready, then exits 0. When the time runs out it exits 1, so the script can stop there.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ docker compose up -d && waitfor localhost:5432 -t 60 && ./migrate.sh
localhost:5432 open after 7.2s
.fi
.RE
.PP
It decides what to wait for from the shape of the argument:
.TP
\fBdb:5432\fR, \fB10.0.0.5:6443\fR, \fB[::1]:8080\fR
A TCP connection to that port succeeds.
.TP
\fB:8080\fR
A TCP connection to \fBlocalhost:8080\fR succeeds.
.TP
\fBhttp://...\fR or \fBhttps://...\fR
The URL answers 200, or the code from \fB\-s\fR.
.TP
Anything else
The path exists. It can be a file, a folder or a socket.
.PP
A port counts as ready when something accepts the connection. \fBwaitfor\fR closes it again at once and sends nothing.
.PP
A URL counts as ready only on the exact code. A 503 while the app starts, a 404 before the route is loaded, or a refused connection all mean "try again". Redirects are not followed, so a URL that answers 301 is ready only with \fB\-s 301\fR, or with \fB\-\- \-L\fR to follow it.
.SH TIMING
.PP
\fBwaitfor\fR tries at once, then once per interval, 1 second by default. It gives up when the timeout has passed, 30 seconds by default. The last try happens at the timeout, so \fB\-t 10\fR really waits 10 seconds.
.PP
Each try has a limit of its own, so one slow try cannot hang the run. A port try waits at most 5 seconds for the connection, and never past the timeout. A URL try passes the same limit to curl as \fB\-\-max\-time\fR.
.PP
\fB\-t 0\fR waits for ever. Use it with care in scripts, since a service that never starts then blocks the script for good.
.PP
Both options take fractions, such as \fB\-i 0.2\fR for five tries a second.
.SH OUTPUT
.PP
The result goes to stdout, as one line:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
db:5432 open after 7.2s
http://localhost:8080/healthz answered 200 after 5.1s
/run/app/app.sock exists after 2.0s
.fi
.RE
.PP
In a terminal, one status line on stderr shows each try while it waits and disappears when it is done. With \fB\-v\fR every try gets a line of its own, with the reason it was not ready:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
try 1  connection refused
try 2  connection refused
.fi
.RE
.PP
When the time runs out, the error says what the last try saw:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
waitfor: gave up after 30s, db:5432 is still closed, connection refused
waitfor: gave up after 30s, http://localhost:8080/ last said 503
waitfor: gave up after 30s, /run/app/app.sock does not exist
.fi
.RE
.PP
The reasons for a port are \fBconnection refused\fR (the host is up and nothing listens), \fBunknown host\fR, \fBno route to host\fR, \fBnetwork unreachable\fR and \fBno answer in Ns\fR (a firewall drops the packets). For a URL they are the HTTP code, \fBrefused\fR, \fBunknown host\fR, \fBno answer in Ns\fR, \fBTLS failed\fR and \fBuntrusted certificate\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-timeout SEC\fR
Give up after SEC seconds. 30 by default. \fB0\fR waits for ever.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interval SEC\fR
Seconds between tries. 1 by default. Fractions such as \fB0.5\fR work.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-status CODE\fR
For a URL, wait for this HTTP code instead of 200.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Only the result line and errors. No status line.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Print the command it runs, then one line per try.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show the help.
.SH PASS\-THROUGH
.PP
Options after \fB\-\-\fR go to curl, and only make sense for a URL. \fBwaitfor\fR refuses them for a port or a path, with exit
.IP \(bu 2
Useful ones:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ waitfor https://localhost:8443/health -- --insecure
$ waitfor http://localhost:8080/api -- -H 'Authorization: Bearer abc123'
$ waitfor http://localhost:8080/ -- -L
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB\-\-insecure\fR accepts a self\-signed certificate, \fB\-H\fR sends a header, and \fB\-L\fR follows redirects.
.SH NEEDS
.PP
\fBbash\fR and \fBtimeout\fR from coreutils for ports. \fBwaitfor\fR opens the connection with Bash's own \fB/dev/tcp\fR, so it needs no \fBnc\fR. \fBcurl\fR for URLs, from the \fBcurl\fR package on every distro. Paths need nothing.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Wait for a database, then migrate
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ waitfor db:5432 -t 60 && ./migrate.sh
db:5432 open after 7.2s
.fi
.RE
.SS Wait for a health check before a smoke test
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ waitfor http://localhost:8080/healthz && ./smoke-test.sh
http://localhost:8080/healthz answered 200 after 5.1s
.fi
.RE
.SS See each try
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ waitfor -v :23456
+ bash -c 'exec 3<>/dev/tcp/localhost/23456'
try 1  connection refused
localhost:23456 open after 1.0s
.fi
.RE
.SS A port that stays closed
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ waitfor -v -t 2 127.0.0.1:23456
+ bash -c 'exec 3<>/dev/tcp/127.0.0.1/23456'
try 1  connection refused
try 2  connection refused
try 3  connection refused
waitfor: gave up after 2.0s, 127.0.0.1:23456 is still closed, connection refused
.fi
.RE
.SS A host that does not exist
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ waitfor -t 1 nonexistent.invalid:80
waitfor: gave up after 1.0s, nonexistent.invalid:80 is still closed, unknown host
.fi
.RE
.SS A socket file
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ waitfor -t 10 /run/app/app.sock
/run/app/app.sock exists after 2.0s
.fi
.RE
.SS An API that answers 401 when it is up
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ waitfor -s 401 http://localhost:8080/api
http://localhost:8080/api answered 401 after 0.0s
.fi
.RE
.SS In a Kubernetes init step or CI job
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
kubectl port-forward svc/web 8080:80 &
waitfor -q -t 20 http://localhost:8080/ || exit 1
curl -s http://localhost:8080/version
.fi
.RE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
\fBis still closed, no answer in 5s\fR : A firewall drops the connection without refusing it. Check the port is open on the host with \fBport PORT\fR there, and in the firewall.
.PP
\fBis still closed, unknown host\fR : The name does not resolve. In Docker Compose, use the service name from inside the network, and \fBlocalhost\fR with the published port from the host.
.PP
\fBlast said 404\fR for a URL you know works : Check the path. \fBwaitfor\fR does not follow redirects, so \fBhttp://localhost:8080\fR and \fBhttp://localhost:8080/\fR may answer differently.
.PP
\fBlast said untrusted certificate\fR : The server uses a self\-signed certificate. Pass \fB\-\- \-\-insecure\fR, or \fB\-\- \-\-cacert ca.pem\fR with its CA.
.PP
The port is open but the service is not ready : A port can accept connections before the service behind it is ready, as with some databases during recovery. Wait for a health URL instead, or run the service's own check, such as \fBpg_isready\fR, after \fBwaitfor\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Ready.
.TP
1
The time ran out before it was ready.
.TP
2
Bad usage, such as no target, a bad number, or \fB\-\-\fR options for a port.
.TP
3
\fBcurl\fR is missing for a URL, or \fBtimeout\fR for a port.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBport\fR, \fBhttptime\fR, \fBnetcheck\fR, \fBcurl(1)\fR, \fBtimeout(1)\fR
